#
# **What a record is, asked once.**
#
# A record is never passed around. It is loaded where it is used and it does not travel: a
# record handed to another object carries the database with it, and whatever holds it can
# lazily load an association, write through it, and reopen a query somewhere nobody is
# looking for one. The call graph refuses a record being *named* out of place; it can say
# nothing about one arriving as an argument, because at that end there is a keyword and no
# constant at all.
#
# Two guards use this, deliberately. `TypedArguments#typed` refuses a record at the moment an
# argument is asserted, which is where the message is most useful — it names the keyword.
# `HoldsNoRecords` sweeps what a presentation object ended up holding, whether or not
# anything was typed, which is the backstop the first cannot be.
#
# **Asked of the loaded object, never of its name.** A repository may call its records
# anything; what makes one a record is its ancestry. `defined?` throughout, because these are
# POROs and this file loads in a suite that never booted Rails.
module Persistence
  def self.record?(value)
    (defined?(::ActiveRecord::Base) && value.is_a?(::ActiveRecord::Base)) ||
      (defined?(::ActiveRecord::Relation) && value.is_a?(::ActiveRecord::Relation))
  end

  # A collection holding one is holding one. Checked to the element because
  # `lines: person.orders` is the shape this arrives in far more often than a bare record.
  def self.anywhere_in?(value)
    case value
    when Array then value.any? { |element| anywhere_in?(element) }
    when Hash then value.any? { |key, element| anywhere_in?(key) || anywhere_in?(element) }
    else record?(value)
    end
  end
end
